// The boss journal: Corvane, the Unburied Blade, last guard of the crater road, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Corvane never speaks, so there are no lines from him. Move keys are cast names (the journal fires on the cast bar's name).
// Whetted Edge is the stance wind-up before every unleashing, where the Whetstone and the three stances live.
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).corvane = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "A sentry on the pass that nobody ever relieved. Every blow of his that landed on one of us put a notch of light on his blade, and a full blade means he cuts loose. So the line kept out of every shadow and every line he drew, and starved it. Held, mostly.",
    gloomsworn: "A guard never relieved. I know the post. Each blow of his that finds one of us lights a notch along his blade, and a full blade is when he cuts loose. We stopped handing him notches. The dead are slow to learn, but we do.",
    thresher: "Such a patient gentleman, standing his post in the snow long after anyone remembered him. Every blow that lands on us puts a light on his blade, and when it is full he lets it all go. Do mind the step, dears, and he never gets his edge.",
    bloodreaver: "The cold one wants me bleeding, and every hit that landed on any of us lit his blade a notch! Four and he cuts loose. The ones meant for me are fair. The ones that catch my friends are gifts to his steel, and I will not give him gifts.",
    runeguard: "The Unburied Blade keeps a plain ledger: each blow of his that lands avoidably adds a notch of light to the steel, and a full blade is released as one of three unleashings. His stance is the omen of which. Not foreseen at first; now it is.",
    gorgemaw: "A cold, severe host, serving one dish over and over, and every plate we failed to dodge seasoned his blade with a notch of light. A full blade and the house special arrives. I kept my plate clean. He is thin fare without it.",
    oathbroker: "A sentinel bound to his post, and bound still, for no one signed his release. Each of his blows that lands avoidably enters a notch on his blade, and a full blade is the clause that lets him unleash. We refused him every notch. So noted, and shelved.",
    ironpalm: "He speaks with a long blade and very few words. Each blow that reached us gave his edge a notch of light, and a full edge made him speak loudly. We learned to be struck less, and he spoke less.",
    ranger: "Old guard, long blade, snow to the knee. He waits for you to miss. Every hit that lands lights a notch on the steel, four and he cuts loose. Watch the stance; it says where the shot comes from. Give it nothing to chew.",
    shadowblade: "The mark stood in the snow as patient as I am, and every blow that landed on us lit a notch along his blade. Four notches and he stopped being patient. Rude, but fair. I gave him nothing to sharpen on, and he never knew I was there.",
    pyromancer: "He is cold! Magnificently, insultingly cold, and every blow of his that landed on any of us lit a notch of light on his blade. Four and he let it all go! I have since learned not to be hit, which I resent on principle. A tidy lesson, if a chilly one.",
    thermist: "Cold all through, and patient. Every hit that landed on us lit a notch along his edge; at four he let it all out. Too far, that. Cool it: give him nothing, and the blade stays dull.",
    driftblade: "He stood in the snow and I ran circles through the pass, never touching down, because every hit that caught any of us lit a notch on his blade and a full blade is bad news. Ran through, ran round, and starved it.",
    marionettist: "A stoic old sentry plays the lead, and he sharpens his blade on our missed cues. Every blow that lands lights a notch, and at four he plays the great scene. The company learned its marks. (The Doll learned them first.)",
    cleric: "A poor soul, left on the pass with no one to tell him he could rest. Every blow that caught one of us lit a notch on his blade, and when it was full he cut loose on all of us. I scolded the careless ones, kindly, and there was a good deal of scolding.",
    grovekeeper: "He stands in the snow like a stump nobody pulled. Every blow that lands lights a notch on his blade, and at the fourth it breaks over us like a late frost. No wandering into his weather, my lot. Give him nothing to grow on.",
    chronomancer: "Every blow of his that lands on someone careless puts a notch on his blade, and at the fourth he unleashes. Next time, which was last time, we gave him nothing to count. He keeps no tempo of his own; we keep it for him.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one dead guard, very cold, with a blade that stores every hit we fail to dodge. Four notches and it discharges. Remedy: do not be hit by what you can see. Label for the shelf: his stance says which discharge.",
    threadweaver: "A tall old soldier in cracked black lacquer, and every blow that lands on one of my stitches puts a notch on his blade. Four notches and it all comes loose at once. Keep out of his way, dears, and don't feed the blade.",
    tidecaller: "He stands in the snow like a tide that went out and never came back. Every blow that caught us lit a notch along his blade, and when it was full he unleashed, so we gave him nothing to catch. There was no hurry in it.",
    lanternkeeper: "A guard at the end of a long road of stone lanterns, none of them lit. Every blow of his that lands on one of us puts a notch of light on his blade, and four fills it. I kept the party out of his way. I'll leave one burning.",
    kenseier: "A swordsman who counts as closely as I do. Every blow of his that finds one of us gives his edge a notch, and the fourth lets him unleash. His stance shows which. A master's courtesy.",
    houndmaster: "Old sentry in the snow, never relieved. Every hit that lands on any of us puts a notch on his blade, and four lets him go. Dogs off his lines and out of his way. Rust learned fast. Stay.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "He is down, and the pass is quiet. Nobody had relieved him, so we did. I stood a moment at his post, at attention, which he had earned. Not one of us was lost.",
    gloomsworn: "He sank into the snow and did not rise. Somebody finally relieved him. I know the weight of a post, and I took off my helm and let him lie.",
    thresher: "His hour came at last, and he went to it so politely. The lanterns are only stone now, and the pass is quiet. Sleep well, dear sentry. His name is off the list.",
    bloodreaver: "The cold one went down, and I have scars from every act of it. A fine duel. I bled for it and I would again. I hope you are properly relieved.",
    runeguard: "The Unburied Blade is laid to rest, and every unleashing is written in the margin with its omen. I allow myself a small pleasure. Let my successor read it with envy, and then with care.",
    gorgemaw: "A lean, cold meal, and I ate every course of it. The pass is silent and I am almost satisfied. He cooked nothing and yet he was the best host in months. Ask me when the cold has left my teeth.",
    oathbroker: "His post is discharged and his watch is ended, and the debt of the crater road is paid in full. The release he was owed was long delayed, and I have filed it late but gladly.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke with the blade, and we answered. His last word was a quiet one. The pass is still. I breathed, and it was the clearest breath of the journey.",
    ranger: "Down. Pass is quiet. Not a clean miss in the last stretch. Walked out past the lanterns and didn't look back. Didn't need to.",
    shadowblade: "He folded into the snow and the pass fell quiet. A mark that kept watch for an age and never once looked behind him. I applauded, softly, from exactly where he was not looking, and not even the end changed that.",
    pyromancer: "The coldest man alive, or dead, whichever, has finally stopped, and I thawed the whole pass with my own two hands! Frostbite I shall boast about for a decade. I would do it again, loudly.",
    thermist: "He's down, and the pass went quiet, and I was warm and cold at once and exactly in the middle! Result: perfect balance. I wrote it on my cuff in case it never happens again.",
    driftblade: "Down he went, and the snow stopped, and I ran out through the broken gate and up the road and kept running, because I could, because nothing was holding me and the sky over the pass was enormous.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain on the Unburied Blade. He took his bow in the snow, as stiff as ever, and the lanterns stood for him like footlights. (The Doll curtseyed. Standing ovation, such as it was.)",
    cleric: "Nobody relieved him for so long, and the light had to come to him in the end. He rests now. Every friend of mine walked down from the pass on their own feet. Thank the light. I counted twice.",
    grovekeeper: "He's down, dear, and the snow will cover him and the spring will come up over the pass. My whole lot walked off it. I have not been so proud since my first garden. Sit. Tea. You have earned the pot.",
    chronomancer: "Someone relieved him at last, which is what he wanted, and it only took the whole fight. His blade stayed dark and not one of us needed undoing. I am rather proud of the notches we never gave him.",
    alembist: "Specimen: at rest, finally. Every sample sealed, only a few frozen to my pockets. Labels: do not thaw until I have forgotten what's in them. Never so cold, never so pleased.",
    threadweaver: "The old soldier is at rest, and his blade is bare of notches at last. I counted my stitches twice. All here. The kettle's on, and nobody is leaving until it boils.",
    tidecaller: "The pass went still and the snow settled, as the sea does when it has finished. Not slack water, which still breathes, but a rest with something under it. I sang over him, low, a little, for his long watch.",
    lanternkeeper: "He went down at the end of the road. I lit the first of his stone lanterns, only one, and left it burning by the shrine for him. Nobody had ever lit them for him.",
    kenseier: "The pass is still. Every stroke had its count, and the last silence was the longest. I sat by the lanterns with the others and said little.",
    houndmaster: "Old sentry's down and the pass has gone quiet. Dogs came out with a bit of frost on their coats and nothing worse. Both pressed in against my legs, steaming. Cold night for a supper.",
  },
  moves: {
    'White Gale': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A gust of snow across the whole pass. Nothing to block, nothing to stand behind. I set my feet, the line closed up near the healer, and we took it together, already mended.",
      gloomsworn: "The gale reached every one of us at once. Wind is not something a man can stand in front of. We came to it whole, close to the healer, and it went through us as it goes through the dead. Cold, mostly.",
      thresher: "He sends a great gust of snow over everyone at once, bless him, and there is no corner to dodge it in. Come to it mended and close together, and do mind your hat.",
      bloodreaver: "A blizzard across the whole pass and not a scar worth keeping. Nothing to dodge, so I stood with the healer and everyone near and let it blow. Cold, but fair.",
      runeguard: "The omen: he lifts the blade and the snow goes still. The answer: be mended and gathered before it breaks, for no ward turns a gale. It reaches every one of us.",
      gorgemaw: "A cold soup poured over every seat at once, and no guest can swallow it for another. We sat close to the healer, came to it already mended, and drank it down. Thin, but bracing.",
      oathbroker: "The gale is a general levy, payable by all together with no exemption for position. The provision is to arrive whole and near the healer, which we did, and the sum was met without default.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke, and snow came with it, to all of us. We were close and we were mended. It passed.",
      ranger: "Gust across the whole pass. Nothing to shelter behind. Mended before, grouped near the healer. Let it howl.",
      shadowblade: "No back to slip behind, no blind side; a gale has none. I stood close to the healer, already mended, and let it blow. Rude of the weather to ignore me entirely.",
      pyromancer: "A gale! Across the whole pass, with all the snow in it, and not one patch of warm ground to hide on! I stayed close to the healer, mended beforehand, and cast straight through it. The snow went sideways, which suits a dramatic exit.",
      thermist: "Cold gust, everyone, no dodge. Mended first, close to the healer, then straight through it. Chilled to the bone, and thawed again before I had finished complaining.",
      driftblade: "The gale caught everyone and there was no running round it, so I ran in close to the healer, whole, and stood nearly still in the wind, which I hate, and it blew past.",
      marionettist: "A great gust of snow across the whole house. No wings to hide in. The company gathered near the healer, mended beforehand, and took it as one. (The Doll wore a scarf. Tasteful.)",
      cleric: "The gale blew over every one of us at once. I gathered my flock close and mended them before it came, not after, and all of them stood up straight in it.",
      grovekeeper: "A gust of snow came over the pass like a hard frost on the whole garden. I'd planted on everyone before it, and my lot stood close, so the mending was already growing.",
      chronomancer: "The gale blows just after he lifts the blade, always. I had everyone mended and close just before, and it passed without anyone being undone.",
      alembist: "Case: a gale across the whole pass, no cover to hide in. Brewed the big dose ahead of it, everyone gathered near me, and nobody mended after. Only one flask froze.",
      threadweaver: "The gale ran along every thread at once. I pulled my stitches in close and mended them before it blew, not after. Nobody drifted anywhere on the wind.",
      tidecaller: "A gust across the whole pass, no shelter anywhere. I raised the water before it came and the others drew in close, so the wind broke on a full shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "The wind went over every one of us, stone lanterns and all. I hung a light on each of us before it blew, and we stood close by the glow. It passed.",
      kenseier: "A gale across the whole pass, and no blade turns the wind. We drew in beside the healer, mended first, and let it blow itself out.",
      houndmaster: "Snow came over the lot of us, dogs and all. No dodging a gale. Whistled the pair in tight beside the healer, got mended before it hit, sat it out.",
    } },
    'Sundering Cut': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The heavy cut came for me alone and left my guard cracked behind it. I waited for the shoulder to drop, took it square, and turned him in the snow so it fell on bare ground, not on the lanterns or the line.",
      gloomsworn: "The cut was meant for me, so I turned him toward bare snow and away from the stone lanterns, which break if the blow reaches them. It split my guard. A wound is only a wound. The rest kept out of the arc.",
      thresher: "The big cut falls on whoever stands in front, and a lantern in its path is broken, so I do hope our shield turns him away from them. The rest of us stay out of the arc. It leaves his guard cracked for the next, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "The first cut split me open and I loved it. It left my guard cracked for the next, which is the one that really hurts. I walked the big one round so it missed the lanterns, and kept my friends out of the arc.",
      runeguard: "He draws back, and a cone opens before the shield. Turn him so it falls on bare snow, not on the lanterns, which shatter if it reaches them, and keep the rest of us out of the arc. The guard cracks after.",
      gorgemaw: "My host's first course, a heavy cut for whoever stands in front. I took it, and turned him so it landed on bare snow, since the lanterns are crockery and break. It cracked my guard. A second course follows. Bland, filling.",
      oathbroker: "The first instalment: a heavy cut, payable by whoever stands in front. The proviso: turn him away from the lanterns, which shatter if the blow reaches them, and the others stay out of the arc. A heavier cut follows.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke the first of two words, a heavy one, to me alone. I turned him from the lanterns so it would not break them, and took it. The others stayed out of its path. Then I braced for the second.",
      ranger: "Heavy cut, aimed at the shield. Stayed clear of the cone, and the shield walked him round so it missed the lanterns. They break if it touches them. The guard cracks; the next one hits harder.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swung at our shield, as it should, and the shield walked it round so the blow missed the lanterns. I stayed well off the cone. The first cut leaves a crack and the second goes through it. Bad form, a second cut in the same place.",
      pyromancer: "He swung at our shield, and the whole cone glowed the colour of trouble. I stayed out of it, and the shield turned him away so the lanterns lived! The guard cracked, and the next one is worse.",
      thermist: "Cold first: the cone in front of the shield, and nothing else worth standing in. Keep clear. The shield turned him from the lanterns. Then the heat, because the crack in the guard means the next blow lands harder.",
      driftblade: "He swung at the shield, so I went round the arc and kept clear while the shield turned him through the snow away from the lanterns, and the cut left a crack behind it that the next blow was sure to find.",
      marionettist: "Act one, scene two: the old sentry cut at the shield. The company stood out of the cone, and the shield turned him so the lanterns, our scenery, stayed standing. (Stage left, a cracked guard. The next blow is worse.)",
      cleric: "The great cut fell on our knight and left his guard cracked. I laid the light on him before it landed, because the second is the heavy one. The rest of us stayed out of its path, and he turned it from the lanterns.",
      grovekeeper: "The cut came down like a branch in a gale, and I'd planted on the shield before it, because the next cut follows the crack. We stood out of the arc, and he turned the old fellow away from the lanterns.",
      chronomancer: "Two cuts, and the first only opens the door for the second. I mended the shield on the first beat and was ready for the heavier one. The rest of us kept out of the arc, and the lanterns were turned away from. Always.",
      alembist: "Two heavy cuts, the second into the crack the first leaves. A dose on the shield before the first, another measured out for the next. Turn him from the lanterns, which shatter like cheap glassware. Everyone else out of the arc.",
      threadweaver: "The cut ran down the thread to our shield and cracked his guard, and the next one aims for the crack. I tied him tight before it fell. We kept clear of the arc, and he walked the old soldier from the lanterns.",
      tidecaller: "A swell with a blade in it, aimed at the shield alone. I raised the tide on him before it broke, for the next swell is the heavier. We stood out of the arc, and he turned it from the lanterns.",
      lanternkeeper: "The cut was for our shield, and a stone lantern in its path would be lost, so he turned the guard away from them. I hung a light on him before it fell and kept the rest of us out of the arc. One for the shield, three for the rest.",
      kenseier: "A heavy cut with a long breath before it. I met it with the blade as it fell, and turned him so it passed over bare snow and not the lanterns. The guard cracked. The others kept out of the arc.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad swung at the shield-man. Dogs stayed well out of the arc. The shield-man walked him round so the blow missed the lanterns; they break if it lands on them. Guard cracks, and the next one hits harder.",
    } },
    'Cracked Guard': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The second blow came hard on the crack the first had made, straight after. I had my shield up in that last instant and the healer's hand on my back already. Everyone else stayed clear of the arc.",
      gloomsworn: "The second cut came into the crack the first had left, and it is the heavier of the two. I drew the dark in whole and took it. The healer had me before the first.",
      thresher: "That second cut does go into the first one's crack, dear, and it is the heavy one. Our shield should be well mended before it and braced. The rest of us stay clear of the arc and let him keep his appointment.",
      bloodreaver: "The second came straight after, hard into the crack in my guard, and I felt every bit of it! Bled first, then drank. Keep the healer ready before it, not after, and my friends out of the arc. A beautiful scar.",
      runeguard: "The crack is the sign itself: the second cut follows the first at once and is the heavier. Lay the strongest ward before the second, not the first, with the healer mending ahead of it.",
      gorgemaw: "The second course, served on the heels of the first, and heavier, into the crack I'd made for it. Brace, swallow, have the healer ready. The others kept out of the arc. A rich finish, if rude.",
      oathbroker: "The second instalment falls due at once and is larger, being charged against the crack the first created. Arrange the heaviest cover for the second, not the first. The others remain outside the arc.",
      ironpalm: "The first word cracked me. The second came into the crack, harder. I held my breath for the first and let it go for the second. The healer was already there.",
      ranger: "Second cut, right into the crack. Harder than the first. Shield braced late, healer early. Others clear of the arc. Steady.",
      shadowblade: "He cut our shield the same way twice, and the second went in through the crack the first left, which is exactly what I'd have done. So the shield braced late and the healer early. I stayed behind him.",
      pyromancer: "The second cut came straight after into the crack in the guard, and the cone glowed twice as bright! Our shield braced late, the healer mended early, and I stayed well out of the arc, looking marvellous.",
      thermist: "The second cut: faster than the first, heavier, into the crack. Brace late, mend early. Out of the arc, everyone else. Then a shiver, quick, and gone.",
      driftblade: "The second came right on the first's heels and into the crack it had made, and the shield braced and the healer had been there since before the first, and the rest of us stayed out of the arc and let them.",
      marionettist: "Act one, scene three: the same cut, twice, and the second goes through the crack the first made. The shield braced late, the healer was in the wings early, and the rest of us stayed off the stage. (A fine reprise.)",
      cleric: "The second cut came right behind the first, into the crack it made, and it is the heavier. I had the light on our knight before the first fell and a second prayer ready for the next. Everyone else stayed out of the arc.",
      grovekeeper: "The second cut follows the first like a frost after a thaw, into the crack. I'd planted on the shield before either, and the mending was growing when it landed. The rest of my lot stayed out of the arc.",
      chronomancer: "The second cut is the one that matters. It comes straight on the first's heels, into the crack. I mended the shield early and braced for the echo. The others stayed out of the arc. I was early. I always am.",
      alembist: "The first cut makes the crack and the second goes in it, harder. The big brew goes on the shield early, before the second lands, and everyone else stays out of the arc. Mending after is mixing too late.",
      threadweaver: "The second cut finds the crack in the first and goes right through it. I tied our shield tight before it and mended after. We stayed out of the arc, and nothing frayed that couldn't be darned.",
      tidecaller: "The second swell comes in on the heels of the first, into the crack it opened, and heavier. I raised the water high on him before either, and kept it climbing. The rest of us stayed out of the arc.",
      lanternkeeper: "The second cut went into the crack the first left, and it is the heavier. I had two lights on our shield by then, one for each blow, and the rest of us stayed out of the arc.",
      kenseier: "The second stroke comes at once, into the crack the first left, and it is the heavier of the two. I met it as it landed, with the guard cracked and the healer ready. The rest kept clear of the arc.",
      houndmaster: "Second cut came straight on the first, into the crack, harder. Shield-man braced late, healer mended early. Rust and Ash stayed out of the arc, both of them.",
    } },
    'Answering Cut': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A quick cut at me alone, lighter than the heavy ones. I took it on the shield, turned him from the lanterns and kept the line out of the cone. On the hard nights the blade came back at once, and I stepped out of the second.",
      gloomsworn: "A short, quick cut, meant for the shield. A wound is a wound, and I took it, but I turned him from the stone lanterns first, for they break. The rest kept out of the cone. On the hard nights it came back, and I stepped out of that one.",
      thresher: "A quick little cut at our shield, dear, nothing heavy, but a lantern in its path would be lost, so do turn him away. The rest of us keep out of the cone. On the hard night the blade comes back at once. Take the first, step out of the second.",
      bloodreaver: "A quick one, aimed at me! It stung and I loved it, and I walked him round so it missed the lanterns and my friends. On the hard nights it came back at me, and I stepped out of the second and made it miss. Take the first, friend.",
      runeguard: "A short cone opens before the shield, light and quick. Take it with the guard, turn him from the lanterns, and keep the rest of us out of the cone. On the hard nights the blade returns at once: the first is taken, the second avoided.",
      gorgemaw: "A small plate, fast, set before the shield. I took it and turned him so it landed on bare snow, since the lanterns are crockery. The rest kept out of the cone. On the hard nights a second helping followed at once, and I stepped aside from that one.",
      oathbroker: "A minor instalment, due quickly and from the shield alone. Turn him from the lanterns, which break if the cut reaches them, and keep the others out of the cone. On the hard nights a second falls due at once. Accept the first, avoid the second.",
      ironpalm: "A short word, said quickly, to the shield. I met it, turned him from the lanterns, and the others stayed out of its way. Breathe. On the hard nights he said it again at once, and I stepped out of that one.",
      ranger: "Quick cut at the shield. Light. Took it, kept him turned off the lanterns, kept the rest of us out of the cone. Hard nights, the blade comes back at once. Stand for the first, leave for the second.",
      shadowblade: "The mark answered our shield with a quick cut, and the shield took it and turned him from the lanterns. I stayed off the cone. On the hard nights he came back for another, and the shield was no longer standing there. Neat.",
      pyromancer: "A quick cut at our shield, and the cone lit up for it! Light, a little, but a lantern in the way would go out, so he turned him. I stayed well clear. On the hard nights it came back, and our shield stepped out of it!",
      thermist: "Cold: a quick cone at the shield, light, and the lanterns break if it reaches them. Turn him. Hot: the rest of us were already out of it. On the hard nights it came back at once, and the shield stepped out.",
      driftblade: "A quick cut at the shield, light enough to shrug off. I ran wide of the cone while he turned the blow from the lanterns. On the hard nights the blade came back for another, and the shield was already gone from where it fell.",
      marionettist: "A brisk little cut at our leading shield, a short scene, and the company stood out of the cone. He turned the blow from the scenery, the lanterns. (The Doll kept well off stage.) On the hard nights, an encore at once, and the shield was gone.",
      cleric: "A quick cut, lighter than the others, aimed at our knight. I laid a little light on him and he turned the blow from the lanterns. The rest of us stayed out of the cone. On the hard nights it came back, and I called him out of the second.",
      grovekeeper: "A quick cut at the shield, like a branch snapping back. I'd planted a little mending on him first, and he turned the blow from the lanterns. The rest of my lot kept out of the cone. On the hard nights it came back, and he stepped out of it.",
      chronomancer: "A quick cut at the shield, lighter than the others, and I mended him a beat ahead of it. He turned the blow from the lanterns, and the rest of us kept out of the cone. On the hard nights it came back, and I called the step before it did.",
      alembist: "A quick, light cut at the shield. A small dose before it, the blow turned from the lanterns, which shatter, and everyone else out of the cone. On the hard nights it returns at once. Take the first, leave the second.",
      threadweaver: "A quick cut ran down the thread to our shield, light, and he turned it from the lanterns so nothing was cut that couldn't be darned. The rest of us kept clear of the cone. On the hard nights it came back, and he stepped out of the seam.",
      tidecaller: "A short swell, quick and light, at the shield. I raised the water a little on him, and he turned the blow from the lanterns. We kept out of the cone. On the hard nights it broke twice, and he stepped out of the second.",
      lanternkeeper: "A quick cut at the shield, and a stone lantern in its path would be lost, so he turned the guard away from them. I hung a small light on him and kept the rest of us out of the cone. On the hard nights it came back. He stepped out of it.",
      kenseier: "A short, quick stroke, honest about its beat, aimed at the shield. I met it as it landed, and turned him so it passed over bare snow. The others kept out of the cone. On the hard nights the stroke came back at once. Leave the second.",
      houndmaster: "Quick cut at the shield-man, a light one. Dogs stayed out of the cone, the shield-man turned him from the lanterns. Hard nights, the blade comes back at once. Take the first, step out of the second.",
    } },
    'Draw Cut': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He drew on one of us, and a long line followed the man down the pass and then stopped. I stepped sideways out of it in the last instant. The rest of the line stayed out of it. Later two came, a breath apart. First, then second.",
      gloomsworn: "The line follows its man until the last moment, then holds still. I stood in it until it settled and stepped aside, the way a man steps out of the road. Nothing else stood in the line either. Later he drew two lines, a breath apart. The second finds you once the first has passed.",
      thresher: "He draws on one of us, and a long white line follows them about, quite devoted, and then stops. Wait for it to settle, then step aside, dear, and do mind the others in the line behind. Later he draws two, one behind the other. Step off the first, then mind the second, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "The line chose me and followed me about like a hound! I let it, and when it stopped I stepped out sideways, and it went through the air where my scar would have been. The others were out of its road. Later two came, one behind the other! First, then second.",
      runeguard: "A line is drawn through one of us, and it follows its mark until a breath before the blade. Wait for it to settle, then step off it, with nobody else in its length. Later, two lines, a breath apart: leave the first, then the second.",
      gorgemaw: "A long thin slice, cut through one guest and carried down the table. It follows its plate until a breath before. I let it settle and stepped aside. The others kept out of its length. A neat cut, too thin. Later two cuts came, one just after the other. A second helping. Mind it.",
      oathbroker: "A line is entered against one of us, and it follows the named party until a moment before it is executed. Step aside once it settles, and every other party is to stay clear of its length. Later, two in sequence: clear of the first, then the second.",
      ironpalm: "He drew a long breath and a long line followed one of us. I did not move until it settled. Then I stepped aside, and it passed. The others were already out of its road. Later he drew two, one after the other. I breathed between them.",
      ranger: "Line follows its mark, then stops. Stepped out sideways when it did. Kept off the line's length. Later: two lines, a breath apart. First, then second.",
      shadowblade: "The mark drew and chose someone else for once. The line followed them about, then stopped, and they stepped out of it. I stayed well off its length. Rude of it to be that obvious. Later two lines, one behind the other. I left the first and was nowhere near the second.",
      pyromancer: "The line followed me about like a lovesick comet, and then it stopped! I stepped out of it just as it went off, and it struck bare ground where I had been, nobody else in its road. Two came later, one trailing the other!",
      thermist: "The line follows its mark, then stops. Step out sideways once it settles. It was gone before I knew it, a streak of steel through the snow I'd been standing in, and my heart ran hot. Later, two lines, one trailing. Leave the first, then the second.",
      driftblade: "The line chose me and followed me round the pass while I ran, and then it stopped, and I went sideways through the gap just as the blade came, and out the other side, and everyone else was off its road already. Later two came, and I slipped out of the gap between them.",
      marionettist: "A spotlight with a blade in it followed one of the company about the stage, then stopped. They stepped out of it on cue. The rest kept clear of its length. (A very long spotlight. Rather rude.) Later a second spotlight follows the first. Step out of one, then the other.",
      cleric: "The line followed one of us, and then it held still, and they stepped out of it just as the blade came. I reminded the others, gently, to keep out of its length. Later he drew two, a breath apart. I called the first and then the second.",
      grovekeeper: "The line followed its mark like a vine up a post, then stopped. They stepped out sideways when it settled, and the rest of my lot stayed out of its length. Easy as pulling a weed, once you know the root. Later two, one behind the other. First, then second.",
      chronomancer: "The line follows its mark until the very last beat, then stands still for one. They stepped out on that beat, and the rest stayed out of its length. I told them early. Later two lines, one a beat behind the other. I told them both.",
      alembist: "A long line, following one of us until just before the blade. Wait for it to settle, step out sideways, and keep everyone else out of its length. Do not run along it; the line is faster. Later: two lines, the second trailing. Leave the first, then the second.",
      threadweaver: "The line followed its mark about like a loose thread, and held still just before it snapped. They stepped out sideways. The rest of my stitches kept clear of its length. A neat dodge. Later two threads, one trailing the other. Step off the first, then the second.",
      tidecaller: "A long thin line followed one of us across the snow like a rip, then went still. They stepped out of it as it settled. The rest of us kept out of its length. It rewards patience. Later two lines, the second a swell behind the first. Step aside, then again.",
      lanternkeeper: "A line followed one of us across the pass and then stopped. I moved the light from beneath their feet and set it down off to the side, and they stepped after it just as the blade came. The rest of us kept clear of its length. Later two. I moved the light aside twice.",
      kenseier: "The line follows its man until a breath before the stroke. I let it settle, then stepped aside. The stroke runs long but it is honest about its beat. The others kept out of its length. Later two strokes, a beat apart. Leave the first, then the second.",
      houndmaster: "Line followed one of us down the pass, then stopped. Stepped out sideways as it settled. Rust and Ash stayed behind me, out of its length. Later two lines, one trailing. Dogs clear of the first, then the second.",
    } },
    'Scabbard Sweep': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He swung the scabbard out to one side and half the pass was marked. We went to the other half, behind his shoulder, before it was struck. Short step, and nobody struck.",
      gloomsworn: "The scabbard pointed left, and the whole left side of the pass was marked. We stood on the right. A short walk, and not the longest I have made. The sweep went by over empty snow.",
      thresher: "He points his scabbard at half the pass, quite politely, and that half is spoken for. We walked to the other, close behind his shoulder, and let him have his sweep.",
      bloodreaver: "The scabbard pointed at half the pass, and I wanted to take it just to see! But the line is shorter than a scar, so I led the others to the other half and let it go over. Rude, to waste a good sweep.",
      runeguard: "The scabbard swings to one side, and the whole side is marked. Take the other half of the room, a short step behind his shoulder, before the blow lands.",
      gorgemaw: "Half the table sliced in one pass, on the side the scabbard points. We moved to the other half and took our seats. The sweep went by over empty plates. An imposing carver, thin on substance.",
      oathbroker: "The scabbard designates the premises: the whole side it points to, forfeit. The opposite half is exempt, and a short step behind his shoulder gets there. We were all on the exempt side.",
      ironpalm: "He swung the scabbard out. He named a side. We moved to the other. The sweep spoke and no one was there to hear it.",
      ranger: "Scabbard points left, half the pass is gone. Moved right, behind the shoulder. Sweep went by over empty snow.",
      shadowblade: "The mark pointed his scabbard at half the pass and told me, very clearly, to be on the other. I was behind his shoulder already, naturally. A mark that points is a mark that tells.",
      pyromancer: "The scabbard swung out and half the pass glowed the colour of trouble! I stopped mid-spell, walked to the dark half behind his shoulder, and finished my cast from there. The other half got the sweep, and I got the view.",
      thermist: "Scabbard to the side. Half the pass marked. Step across, behind his shoulder, to the quiet side. Then the rush of it, because I could have been caught and wasn't.",
      driftblade: "The scabbard swung out and half the pass was marked, so I cut through the middle behind his shoulder and out onto the quiet side while the sweep went by over the empty snow, which is how it should always be.",
      marionettist: "He pointed the scabbard, and half the stage was marked for the sweep. The company moved upstage to the other half, behind his shoulder, on cue. (Applause, mostly for the Doll, who got there first.)",
      cleric: "He pointed the scabbard to one side, and I called everyone to the other, behind his shoulder, before the sweep came. A short walk, and not a hair harmed. Small mercies.",
      grovekeeper: "The scabbard swung out like a gate, and one half of the pass was shut. We walked to the open half, behind his shoulder, and the sweep went over bare ground. My lot know to read the weather now.",
      chronomancer: "The scabbard points, and then the sweep, always in that order. We were on the quiet half before the first beat of it. I began to warn them, but they had already moved.",
      alembist: "Half the pass is swept, the half the scabbard points to. Cross to the other half, a short step behind his shoulder, before the blow. Crossing late is adding the catalyst after the flash.",
      threadweaver: "The scabbard swung out and half the pass was cut. I drew my stitches over to the other half, behind his shoulder, before the blow. Nothing snagged. A neat hem, if I say so.",
      tidecaller: "The scabbard pointed to one side like a headland, and half the pass was where the surf would break. We walked to the other half, behind his shoulder, well before. The sweep went over empty snow.",
      lanternkeeper: "The scabbard pointed to one side and half the pass was marked. I carried my light across to the other half, behind his shoulder, and set it down there. They followed it. It was already burning when they arrived.",
      kenseier: "The scabbard swings to one side and the other side is safe. A sweep has a beat, and we crossed to the quiet half behind his shoulder before it landed. A courteous warning, for a dead man.",
      houndmaster: "Scabbard pointed left, whole left half of the pass marked. Called the dogs across the far side behind his shoulder. Sweep went over empty snow.",
    } },
    'Still Water': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He planted his feet, and the snow before him went glassy. Anyone on it is countered, so nobody stood before him. We struck from his sides and back until he moved. The line kept its ground.",
      gloomsworn: "He took a parry stance and the floor before him turned to glass. Anything standing on it is struck back. So none of us did. A man who has been buried does not need the best seat.",
      thresher: "He holds himself so perfectly still, and the snow in front of him goes glassy, and anyone on it is cut back. So nobody stood in front, bless him. We worked from his sides, and he stayed put, quite lost.",
      bloodreaver: "He stood still and the snow in front of him went to glass, and anything that stepped on it got hit right back. I wanted to! But the others would have taken the wounds. So we all kept to his sides and his back.",
      runeguard: "He sets his feet, and the floor before him goes glassy. It counters whoever stands on it, so no one does; strike from the flanks and the back until he moves.",
      gorgemaw: "A parry stance, a glassy plate laid before him. Whoever eats from it is bitten back. We dined from the flanks and the rear instead. A thin dish, well defended.",
      oathbroker: "The terms of the stance: any party entering the glassy floor before him is countered at once. The flanks and the rear lie outside the clause, and every party worked from there until he moved.",
      ironpalm: "He went still, and the floor before him turned to glass. To step in is to be answered. We did not step in. We spoke from the side until he moved.",
      ranger: "Parry stance. Glass on the floor in front. Anyone on it gets struck back. Flank or rear, never front. Shot from there.",
      shadowblade: "The mark held itself perfectly still and the floor in front of it turned to glass, and anyone on it is struck back. So I stayed behind it, where I belong. A stance like that sees nothing it is not facing.",
      pyromancer: "He froze like a statue and the floor before him shone like glass, and anything on it was struck back! I'd never been so dazzled. I cast from his flank and kept clear. Rather chilly, and quite magnificent.",
      thermist: "Parry stance. Glass floor in front, countered if stepped on. Flanks and behind only. I wanted to dash across it, wanted it badly, and didn't.",
      driftblade: "He stood perfectly still and the floor before him turned to glass, which I'd never cross, and so I ran circles round his flank and back and stayed off the front the whole time, and he never moved, the old statue.",
      marionettist: "Our star held a pose, and the stage before him turned to glass. Whoever steps on it is struck back. The company worked the wings and the back of the set. (Stage fright, from the front row.)",
      cleric: "He stood still as a stone, and the floor before him went glassy. Whoever stepped on it was struck back. I reminded everyone to stay at his sides and behind, and we did, until he moved.",
      grovekeeper: "He stood like a frozen pond, and the ground before him turned to glass. Anyone setting foot on it gets bitten back. We kept to the sides and the back until the thaw. My lot learned that quickly.",
      chronomancer: "He holds one beat for a long time, and the floor before him is glass, and countering whatever steps on it. We kept to his flanks and rear until the beat ended. I've known that tempo all along.",
      alembist: "A parry stance, glass on the floor before him. Nobody stands on the glass; flanks and rear until he moves. Thrown reagents land just as well from the side.",
      threadweaver: "He went still and the snow before him turned to glass, and a foot on it is struck back at once. I kept my stitches to his flanks and back, and every one stayed whole. No stepping on the glass, I told them, and I meant it.",
      tidecaller: "He held perfectly still, the way a lake does before ice. The floor before him went to glass, and what steps on it is struck back. We stayed along his flanks and behind, and waited for the thaw.",
      lanternkeeper: "He stood so still and the floor before him went glassy, and whoever stepped on it was struck back. I lit the flanks and the rear for us instead, and hung a light on no one in front.",
      kenseier: "A parry stance, and a better one than most. The floor before him is glass, and a blow into it is answered. We took the flanks and the rear and let him wait. A stance the blade cannot cross, only the sides.",
      houndmaster: "Big lad stood still as a post and the floor before him went glassy. Anything stepping on it gets bit back. Held the dogs off it, worked his flanks and back.",
    } },
    'Hollow Breath': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cold circle followed our healer about and bit whoever stood in it, five breaths running. The line kept off the healer and the healer drew it wide of us. No one stood near.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring of cold followed the healer, five breaths in a row, and bit whoever stood too close. The healer took it out to the edge, and the rest of us gave her room. A man alone gets used to that.",
      thresher: "A cold ring follows the healer about for five breaths, and bites anyone who stands near, so the healer wandered to the edge, quite alone, humming, and the rest of us kept clear.",
      bloodreaver: "A ring of cold on the healer, five breaths of it, and it bites anyone who stands near. Not my kind of wound! I kept clear of the healer, who walked it off to the edge. They deserve a quieter breath.",
      runeguard: "A ring appears about the healer and follows them, five times. The healer goes to the edge alone, and none of us stand within its reach.",
      gorgemaw: "A cold ring around the healer, five bites, and everyone near is on the menu. The healer walked off to the edge to dine alone, and the rest of us kept clear. Underseasoned, and lonely.",
      oathbroker: "A levy of cold is placed upon the healer's person and any party within its ring, renewable five times. The healer attends the edge alone, and everyone else remains outside it.",
      ironpalm: "A ring followed the healer, five times, and spoke coldly to all within. The healer walked to the edge alone. We stayed away. We all breathed easier for it.",
      ranger: "Ring of cold on the healer, follows, five times. Healer to the edge, alone. Rest keep out of reach. Easy shot, from there.",
      shadowblade: "A cold ring followed the healer about and bit anyone near. The healer went to the edge, alone and quite dramatic, and I kept my distance. Nobody follows me, which is how I like it.",
      pyromancer: "A ring of cold on the healer, five times, with everyone near it frosted! I stayed well away, from fear of being hurt and fear of being cold. The healer walked it to the edge. Heroic, and I'd say so to her face.",
      thermist: "Cold ring on the healer, five breaths. Everyone near it is bitten. The healer goes to the edge, alone. The rest clear. I was nearly in it, heart going, and then I wasn't.",
      driftblade: "A ring of cold followed the healer round the pass for five breaths and I ran well clear of it, wide, and through the gaps between, and the healer took it out to the edge, alone, and kept mending.",
      marionettist: "A cold spotlight followed our healer about the stage, five times, and bit whoever stood in it. She took it to the wings. The company kept well clear of her. (The Doll wore a coat.)",
      cleric: "A ring of cold followed me for five breaths, and bit whoever came near. I walked it out to the edge, away from everyone, and kept the light on them from there. A long reach is a mercy.",
      grovekeeper: "A ring of frost followed me about like a hard winter, five times, and bit anyone close. I took it to the edge alone, and kept planting from there. My lot kept clear, like sensible saplings.",
      chronomancer: "The ring follows me, a beat behind, five times, and bites anyone near. I walked it to the edge, and the rest kept well clear. I told them to move. They did, early.",
      alembist: "A cold ring on the healer, five breaths, biting everyone within. The healer takes it to the edge, alone, and brews from there. The rest stay out of reach. Distance is the best stopper.",
      threadweaver: "A ring of cold followed me about for five breaths, and bit anyone in it. I drew my stitches loose, walked it out to the edge, and mended from there on a long thread.",
      tidecaller: "A ring of cold followed me like a rip, five breaths, and bit whoever stood in it. I walked out to the edge, alone, and sang from there, and the others kept well back.",
      lanternkeeper: "A cold ring followed me about, five breaths, and bit anyone near. I carried my lanterns out to the edge, alone, and lit them from there, and the rest kept their distance.",
      kenseier: "A breath of cold followed the healer, five times, and a blade cannot turn it. The healer walked it to the edge. The rest of us stayed out of its reach.",
      houndmaster: "Cold ring on the healer, five breaths, and it bites whatever stands close. Healer walked it to the edge. Rust and Ash stayed well clear of her.",
    } },
    'Whetted Edge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "When his edge is full he sets his stance, and the stance names the blow. Blade low and back, a fan; raised high, a dash; sunk in the snow, the dance. I read it before the floor showed and had the line moving.",
      gloomsworn: "The edge fills, and he stands and shows which blow it will be: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. I read the stance and moved before the floor said a word. Every notch of it was ours to refuse him.",
      thresher: "When his edge is full he strikes a pose, dear, and the pose is the whole programme: blade low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. Read it, move on it, and don't wait for the floor.",
      bloodreaver: "The edge filled and he struck his pose, and the pose says which blow is coming! Low and back, up high, or planted in the snow. I learned the three and moved on the pose. Every notch was a gift from us. Stop giving.",
      runeguard: "The omen of omens: when the edge is full he takes a stance, and it names the blow. Low and back, the fan; overhead, the dash; sunk in the snow, the dance. The answer is to move on the stance, before the floor.",
      gorgemaw: "The edge fills and he plates the menu before the course: blade low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. Read the card and move before the dish arrives. Every notch was one we served him ourselves.",
      oathbroker: "The notice of intent: when the edge is full, a stance is entered, and it names the instrument. Low and back, overhead, or sunk in the snow. Act on the notice, not on the floor.",
      ironpalm: "The edge filled, and he stood to speak. Low and back, raised high, sunk in the snow: each stance is a different word. I heard it before it was said and moved on it.",
      ranger: "Edge full, he takes a stance. Low and back, high, or sunk in the snow. Each one is a different shot. Read it and move before the floor shows. Every notch was ours to deny him.",
      shadowblade: "The edge filled and the mark took his pose, and the pose gave away everything. Low and back, raised high, sunk in the snow. I knew where to be before the floor did.",
      pyromancer: "His edge filled and he struck a pose, and the pose told me the whole show! Low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. I moved on the pose, well before the floor lit. Every notch was my own doing.",
      thermist: "Edge full. His stance names the blow. Low and back, raised high, sunk in the snow. Move on the stance, not the floor. Every notch of that was ours, and it burned to know it. Give him fewer.",
      driftblade: "The edge filled and he took his stance, low and back, or raised high, or sunk in the snow, and each one told me where to run before the floor had shown a thing, so I was already through it when it did.",
      marionettist: "When his edge is full, our star takes his mark and holds the pose before the scene: blade low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. The company reads the pose and moves on the cue, not the lights. (Every notch was ours.)",
      cleric: "When his edge is full he stands and shows which blow it will be: blade low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. I called everyone to move on the stance, before the floor showed. Every notch was our own doing, I'm afraid.",
      grovekeeper: "The edge fills like a bud, and his stance tells you what will bloom: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. Read it and move on it. Don't wait for the floor. Every notch was ours to withhold.",
      chronomancer: "The edge fills, and his stance shows well ahead of the floor which blow is coming: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. We moved on the stance. Early, as I like it. The notches were ours.",
      alembist: "A full edge, and the cause is every hit we failed to dodge. Read the stance, low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow, and move before the floor shows. Stance first, floor second.",
      threadweaver: "The edge filled and he took his stance, and the stance names the blow: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. I told my stitches to move on the stance, not the floor. Every notch was a snag of ours.",
      tidecaller: "When his edge is full he gathers himself like a swell, and the way he stands tells which wave: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. We moved on the stance, before the floor showed. Every notch was ours.",
      lanternkeeper: "The edge filled and he took his stance, and the stance says which blow: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. I carried our light to the right ground before the floor showed anything. Every notch was ours to refuse.",
      kenseier: "When the edge is full he takes a stance, and a stance has a beat. Low and back, raised high, sunk in the snow. Read it and move on it, before the floor. A courtesy, for a dead man. Every notch was ours to refuse.",
      houndmaster: "Edge full, and he takes a stance that names the blow: low and back, raised high, or sunk in the snow. Moved the lot of us on the stance, dogs too, before the floor showed. Every notch was ours.",
    } },
    'Rising Crane': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A wide fan of steel at one of us, then a second turned across it. The only safe ground is behind him. The line went there at once and stayed put.",
      gloomsworn: "A wide fan, then another turned across it. Behind him is the only quiet place, so we stood there. The dead are used to standing behind things.",
      thresher: "The Crane, dear: a great fan of steel, and then another turned across the first. Go behind him, past the blade, and stay there for both.",
      bloodreaver: "A big fan of steel toward one of us, and another turned across it! I wanted to ride into it and I didn't. Behind him is the only quiet ground, so we all stood there.",
      runeguard: "The omen is the fan; its answer is behind him. A wide fan comes, then a second turned across it, and only the ground at his back escapes both.",
      gorgemaw: "A fan of steel, then another turned across it, a double serving for one table. The only seats left are behind the host. We took them and watched the platter pass.",
      oathbroker: "The clause: a wide fan against one named party, followed by a second turned across it. The only exempt ground is behind him, and we occupied it.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke a wide word, then another, turned across the first. The only quiet was behind him. We stood there, and both words passed over us.",
      ranger: "Wide fan, then a second turned across it. Only safe ground is behind him. Got there before the floor showed.",
      shadowblade: "The mark turned on one of us with a great fan of steel, then another across it. Behind him, as always. The back is the one place a mark cannot watch.",
      pyromancer: "A great fan of steel at one of us and a second one right across it! I went behind him, as one does, and went on casting, and both fans went round me.",
      thermist: "A fan, then another across it. The only safe ground is behind him. I was already running, breathless, and arrived at the one spot that was quiet.",
      driftblade: "The fan swung toward one of us and then another came across it, so I slipped behind him, out of both, and round, and he never turned.",
      marionettist: "The Crane: a double sweep of the stage, the second turned across the first. The company moved upstage, behind him. Both sweeps passed over an empty set. (Applause.)",
      cleric: "A wide fan, and then another across it, and I called everyone behind him at once, where both fell short. Not one of us was caught.",
      grovekeeper: "A fan of steel, then another across it, like a branch bent for the snap. The only ground out of both is behind him. My lot went there, and the fans passed over.",
      chronomancer: "The Crane is two fans, the second a quarter-turn after the first. We went behind him on the first beat and stayed there through the echo. I will have known that all along.",
      alembist: "A wide fan, then a second across it. Go behind him, past the blade, before the floor lights. There is one safe place, and it is his back.",
      threadweaver: "A fan of steel, and another turned across it. I drew my stitches round behind him, past the blade, and the fans passed over us. A neat bit of needlework.",
      tidecaller: "A wide fan, then a second turned across it, like a wave pulling out before it breaks. We went behind him, and the water ran past, and the fan spent itself on an empty shore.",
      lanternkeeper: "I carried our light round behind him and set it down, and they followed. Both fans passed over empty snow, and the light was waiting when they came.",
      kenseier: "A wide fan, then a second turned across it, and no blade turns either. The only ground out of both is behind him. We crossed behind on the beat.",
      houndmaster: "A great fan swung at one of us, then another across it. Only safe ground's behind him. Whistled the dogs round. Both fans went over empty snow.",
    } },
    'Comet Draw': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The blade raised high: a dash through whoever stood farthest from him, and on to the wall. A lantern between him and the man shelters him, once, and cracks. The others stayed clear of the line. Then he turned at the wall and drew back down the pass. I stepped off that line too.",
      gloomsworn: "Blade raised overhead, and a dash through the one farthest from him. A stone lantern in the line shelters you, once, and then it cracks. I watched the farthest of us walk into the dark behind one. Even I hide from some things. At the wall he turned and drew again. A second blow follows.",
      thresher: "Blade raised high, dear, and off he goes through whoever stands farthest from him. A stone lantern in the line will shelter its man once, and then it breaks. The rest stay out of the line. Mind, dears: at the wall he turns and draws again, so step aside twice.",
      bloodreaver: "Blade up high and he came through the farthest one of us like a bolt! I'd have taken it, but a lantern did the job instead, and cracked for it. Stand behind one if you're the farthest, and everyone out of the line. At the wall he turned and drew back! Sidestep that too.",
      runeguard: "The blade is raised overhead, and he runs through whoever stands farthest from him, to the wall. The farthest hides in a lantern's shadow, which shelters once and breaks, and nobody else stands in the line. At the wall he draws back. Step off that too.",
      gorgemaw: "The blade raised overhead, then a dash through the farthest guest, clean to the wall. A stone lantern holds the plate once and then cracks. The farthest of us stood behind one. The line was empty otherwise. Neat service. And at the wall, a second course: a cut back down the table. Step aside again.",
      oathbroker: "Stance: blade overhead. Term: he runs through the party farthest from him, to the wall. The shelter clause: a stone lantern covers its party once and is then void. The farthest took cover in one; the others stayed clear. Rider clause: at the wall he draws back. Stand clear of that too.",
      ironpalm: "The blade went up. He spoke a long word through the one farthest away. A lantern's shadow stood between them, once, and cracked. The rest of us kept out of the line. At the wall he turned and drew back. I stepped aside twice.",
      ranger: "Sword raised. He runs through whoever's farthest. Lantern in the line shelters you, once, then cracks. Farthest man took cover. Everyone else kept clear of the line. Wall, turn, second line back down the pass. Step off that too.",
      shadowblade: "Blade overhead, and he chose the farthest of us, which is never me. The farthest hid in a lantern's shadow, which shelters once and breaks, and the dash went through. I stayed off the line, as ever. At the wall he turned and drew back. I was already somewhere else.",
      pyromancer: "Blade overhead, and then a bolt through the farthest of us, to the wall! The farthest stood in a lantern's shadow and it cracked just after, and the rest of us were nowhere near the line. Dazzling, really. Then he turned at the wall and drew again! Step aside twice, darlings.",
      thermist: "Blade up. He runs through the farthest of us. A lantern's shadow shelters once, and the farthest took cover behind one. Everyone else, off the line, fast. The lantern cracked behind us. At the wall he turns and draws back. Step off that too.",
      driftblade: "The blade went up and he came through the farthest of us to the wall in one run, and the farthest of us was hiding behind a lantern, which shelters once and cracks, and I was off the line and round it already. At the wall he drew back; I ran off that line too.",
      marionettist: "Blade overhead, and a grand dash through the farthest of the company, clear to the wall. A stone lantern in the line shelters once and cracks, like a prop. The rest stood well off the line. (A breathtaking entrance.) At the wall he turns for an encore, a line drawn back. Step aside again.",
      cleric: "The blade went up, and he ran through whoever stood farthest from him. A lantern in the line sheltered our friend, once, and cracked. I reminded the rest of us to keep out of the line. At the wall he turned and drew back. I told them gently to step aside twice.",
      grovekeeper: "Blade up like a branch before a snap, and he ran through the farthest of us. A stone lantern in the line sheltered its man, once, and split. My lot stood well off the line. The farthest knew where to stand. At the wall he turned and drew again. Step aside twice, my lot.",
      chronomancer: "Blade up, and a beat later he is through the farthest of us and at the wall. The lantern sheltered its man, once, and cracked. I'd have known which lantern. The rest kept off the line. At the wall he turns and draws back. I stepped off that one too, early.",
      alembist: "Blade overhead, a dash through whoever is farthest, to the wall. The farthest takes cover behind a stone lantern, which shelters once and cracks; everyone else out of the line. Shelf note: lanterns are consumable. At the wall he draws again, back down the pass. Step off twice.",
      threadweaver: "Blade up, and he ran through the farthest of my stitches to the wall. A stone lantern sheltered that one, once, and cracked, and the rest of us stayed well off the line. A close seam, and clean. At the wall he turned and drew back. A second seam to step out of.",
      tidecaller: "Blade raised high like a wave at its crest, and he ran through the farthest of us. A stone lantern in the line sheltered its man, once, and cracked. The rest of us kept off the line. At the wall he turned and drew back, the tide returning. Step aside twice.",
      lanternkeeper: "He raised the blade and ran through the one farthest from him. A stone lantern stood in the way and sheltered them, once, and cracked, and I was sorry for it. The rest of us kept out of the line. At the wall he drew back. I moved the light twice.",
      kenseier: "The blade raised high, and a dash through the farthest of us to the wall. A stone lantern in the line sheltered its man once, and broke. The rest kept off the line. A courteous warning, then a long stroke. At the wall he turns and draws back. The second stroke keeps its own beat.",
      houndmaster: "Blade up high, and he ran through the farthest of us, right to the wall. Farthest man stood behind a stone lantern, which shelters once and cracks. Rust and Ash stayed well off the line. At the wall he turned and drew back down the pass. Dogs off that line too.",
    } },
    'Hundredfold Parting': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Blade into the snow, and he went to each of us in turn, leaving a numbered image where we stood. One cut ran through them all, in order. The line got clear of every stripe of it.",
      gloomsworn: "He drove the blade into the snow and walked to each of us, leaving a likeness where we stood. One cut joined them in order. I stepped off the lines. The images are hollow, as I am.",
      thresher: "He calls on each of us in turn, dear, and leaves a numbered silver likeness where we stood. One cut then joins them all in order. Step off every one of the lines.",
      bloodreaver: "He called on each of us and left a ghost where we stood, and then one cut ran down the row! I got clear of it and it hit the snow instead. Rude, to waste a good cut. A pretty dance, though.",
      runeguard: "The blade is sheathed in the snow, and he visits each of us and leaves a numbered image. One cut joins them in order. Stand off every line.",
      gorgemaw: "He called on each guest in turn and left a silver place-setting where we sat, and one cut ran through every setting in order. We left our seats and stood clear of the knife. Tiresome, but rich.",
      oathbroker: "The instrument: he visits each party in turn, entering a numbered image where they stood, and one cut joins the entries in order. Each party stands clear of every line.",
      ironpalm: "The blade went into the snow. He went to each of us, one by one, and left an image. One long word then joined them all. We stepped off its path.",
      ranger: "Blade in the snow. He blinks to each in turn, leaves a numbered image. One cut joins them in order. Stand off every line and the cut finds nothing.",
      shadowblade: "He planted the blade and went to each of us in turn, leaving a silver shadow where we stood, which I thought was in poor taste. One cut ran through them all in order. I stepped aside.",
      pyromancer: "He stuck the blade in the snow and blinked to each of us in turn, leaving a silver likeness, and not a flattering one! One cut joined them all. I stepped off the lines, cursing.",
      thermist: "Blade in the snow. He visits each of us, leaves a numbered image, then one cut joins them. Off the lines. The cut came and I was already gone, laughing a little.",
      driftblade: "He planted the blade and blinked to each of us in turn and left a silver ghost where we stood, and I stepped off the lines and through the gap between two of them while the cut ran by.",
      marionettist: "The grand finale: blade in the snow, and our star calls on each of the company in turn, leaving a silver understudy where they stood. One cut joins them all in order. The company stepped off the lines.",
      cleric: "He planted the blade and went from one of us to the next, leaving a silver image where each stood. Then one cut ran through them all, in order. I led everyone off the lines.",
      grovekeeper: "Blade in the snow like a stake, and he walked a row through us, leaving a silver seedling at each of our feet. One cut ran through every one, in order. We stepped off the rows.",
      chronomancer: "Blade in the snow: he visits each of us in turn, then one cut runs through the images in order. We stepped off the lines before the cut.",
      alembist: "Blade in the snow, a visit to each of us, numbered images, then one cut through them all in order. Stand off every line, and the cut through the images breaks nothing of ours.",
      threadweaver: "He planted the blade and went to each of my stitches in turn, leaving a silver tack where they stood, and then one line was pulled through the lot in order. We stepped off. A tidy escape.",
      tidecaller: "Blade sheathed in the snow, and he went from one of us to the next, leaving silver images like pools the tide forgot. Then one cut ran through them all in order. We stepped off the lines.",
      lanternkeeper: "Blade in the snow, and he walked to each of us and left a silver image where we stood. One cut then joined them in order. I picked our light up and set it down off every line, and they followed.",
      kenseier: "The blade in the snow, and then he visited each of us in order, leaving an image. A single stroke runs through them all, in order. We stood off every line. A master's flourish.",
      houndmaster: "Blade in the snow, and he went to each of us in turn, leaving a silver ghost where we stood. One cut joined the lot, in order. Called the dogs off every line.",
    } },
    'Cold Sheathe': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He sheathed the blade and the cold went through every one of us at once, after each unleashing. Nothing to block. The line closed up near the healer, already mended.",
      gloomsworn: "The blade went home and the cold reached every one of us, every time he finished. I made sure the watch was whole before it came. It passed through us like through a gate.",
      thresher: "When he puts the blade away, a great chill goes through all of us, dear, so come to it mended and close together. It comes after every one of his big moments.",
      bloodreaver: "He sheathed it and the cold hit every one of us at once! Nothing to dodge, so I drank what I had bled and stayed close to the healer. It comes after every great blow. Fair trade.",
      runeguard: "The blade goes home, and the cold follows at once, reaching all of us. Be mended and gathered beforehand, every time he finishes an unleashing.",
      gorgemaw: "The sheathing is a cold digestif poured for all of us after each great course. We came to it mended and close, and drank it down. Thin, bitter, and over quickly.",
      oathbroker: "The closing levy: after every unleashing, a general cold is owed by all, jointly. Arrive whole, and near the healer. That was observed.",
      ironpalm: "He sheathed the blade, and the cold spoke to all of us. We were whole and close. It was over in a breath.",
      ranger: "Blade away, cold through everyone. After every unleashing. Mended before, grouped. No shelter in it. It passes.",
      shadowblade: "He sheathed it and the cold found everyone, as bluntly as a door. Be whole beforehand, and close to the healer. A rude way to end a performance.",
      pyromancer: "He put the blade away and a great cold went through every one of us at once! I stood close to the healer, already mended, and kept my hands warm. Frosty, but fair.",
      thermist: "Blade away, cold through everyone, every time. Mended and near the healer before it, always. Then the chill went, and I was warm again by the next breath.",
      driftblade: "He sheathed it and the cold came through all of us and I couldn't run from it, so I ran in close to the healer, whole, and let it pass.",
      marionettist: "The blade goes home, and a cold curtain falls on the whole house after every great scene. The company gathered close and came to it mended. (A chilly bow.)",
      cleric: "He put the blade away and the cold went through every one of us. I mended before it came, not after, and kept everyone close.",
      grovekeeper: "The blade goes home and the frost comes over everything, every time. I planted before it, and my lot stayed close.",
      chronomancer: "The blade goes home and the cold comes just behind it, every time. We were mended and close just before. I was early, as I like to be.",
      alembist: "After every unleashing, a cold through the whole party. Everyone close, and the big brew down before, not after.",
      threadweaver: "The cold ran along every thread when he put the blade away. I pulled my stitches in close and had them mended before, not after.",
      tidecaller: "When the blade goes home, a chill goes through every one of us, each time. I brought the water up beforehand and the others drew in close.",
      lanternkeeper: "The cold went through every one of us when the blade went home, after every unleashing. I hung a light on each of us before, and we stood close.",
      kenseier: "The sheathing is no blow a blade can turn. It reaches everyone, and comes after each unleashing. We stood close, mended beforehand, and outlasted it.",
      houndmaster: "Blade goes home, cold goes through the lot of us, every time. Whistled the dogs in tight by the healer, mended before.",
    } },
    'The Vigil': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He knelt, and a cold wisp rose from each broken lantern, that lantern's own light. One cut down before it reached him flew home and lit its lantern again. One that arrived gave his blade a notch. Cover and a dull edge, both for the striking.",
      gloomsworn: "He knelt, and the broken lanterns sent their lights drifting to him. A wisp struck down on the way flies home and lights its lantern again; one that reaches him sharpens his blade. We struck them all. He rose when he chose, wisps or none.",
      thresher: "He kneels, bless him, and every broken lantern sends up its little light to him. Strike one down on the way and it flies home and relights its lantern, dear; let one arrive and his blade takes a notch. He rises in his own time, so cut them all.",
      bloodreaver: "He knelt, and the broken lanterns sent their wisps drifting at him! Cut one down and it flies home and lights its lantern again, which means cover later. Let one reach him and his blade gets a notch. I cut every one. He rose on his own anyway.",
      runeguard: "He kneels, and each broken lantern sends up its light toward him. Strike each wisp before it arrives; it flies home and relights its lantern, restoring cover, while one that arrives adds a notch. He rises at his own hour, not ours.",
      gorgemaw: "He knelt, and each broken lantern sent its small cold light to the table. Strike one before it arrives and it flies home and lights its lantern again, a plate returned to the shelf. Let one through and the blade takes a notch. He rose when the course was over.",
      oathbroker: "A vigil is declared: each broken lantern dispatches its light to him. A wisp struck down on the way returns home and relights its lantern, restoring cover; a wisp delivered adds a notch to his blade. The vigil ends on his schedule.",
      ironpalm: "He knelt, and the broken lanterns sent their lights toward him. I struck each before it reached him, and it flew home and lit its lantern again. One that arrives is a word for his blade. He rose when he rose.",
      ranger: "He kneels. Broken lanterns send wisps at him, one each. Shoot one on the way and it flies home, relights its lantern: cover back. One that arrives, a notch. He rises on his own, wisps or none. Shot every one.",
      shadowblade: "He knelt, and the broken lanterns sent their lights to him one by one. I cut each down on the way, and each flew home and lit its lantern again, which is cover I shall want. One that arrives sharpens him. He rose regardless.",
      pyromancer: "He knelt and every broken lantern sent up a wisp of its own light! Burn one on the way and it flies home and relights its lantern, and cover returns! One that arrives gives his blade a notch. He rose when he pleased.",
      thermist: "He kneels. Each broken lantern sends a wisp at him. Strike one on the way and it flies home and relights its lantern. One that arrives is a notch. I struck them all, hands flying. He rises on his own, regardless.",
      driftblade: "He knelt and the broken lanterns sent their lights drifting at him, and I ran at each through the snow and cut it before it got there, and it flew home and lit its lantern again, and the next one, and the next, and then he rose.",
      marionettist: "Interval: our star kneels, and every broken lantern sends its light across the stage to him. Strike it on the way and it flies home and relights its lantern, props restored. One that arrives, a notch. He rises on his own cue.",
      cleric: "He knelt, and a pale light rose from each broken lantern and drifted to him. Strike one down and it goes home and lights its lantern again; let one reach him and his blade is sharper. We struck every one. He rose when he chose.",
      grovekeeper: "He knelt, and each broken lantern sent up its light like seed on the wind. Pull one before it lands and it flies home and relights its lantern, so there's cover for the spring. One that lands gives his blade a notch. He rose in his season.",
      chronomancer: "He kneels, and each broken lantern sends its light after him, a beat apart. Strike one on the way and it flies home and lights its lantern again; one that arrives is a notch. He rises on his own beat. We struck them all, and I'd already known.",
      alembist: "He kneels while the broken lanterns each send a light drifting his way. Catch every wisp early and it goes home to relight its lantern, which brings the cover back. A wisp that gets through adds a notch to the blade. He stands when he likes, so don't wait for the lot.",
      threadweaver: "He knelt, and every broken lantern sent a little cold thread of light after him. Snip one on the way and it flies home and lights its lantern again; let one arrive and the blade gets a notch. He rises in his own time.",
      tidecaller: "He knelt, and the pass went quiet as an ebb, and each broken lantern sent its pale light drifting after him. Strike one on the way and it flies home and relights its lantern; one that arrives sharpens his blade. He rose when the water turned.",
      lanternkeeper: "He knelt, and each broken lantern sent its light drifting to him, and each was a lantern of mine to light again. I struck every wisp on the way, and each flew home and relit its lamp. One that arrives sharpens his blade. He rose on his own.",
      kenseier: "He knelt, and each broken lantern sent its light to him, each with a slow beat. Strike one before it arrives and it flies home and lights its lantern again; one that arrives gives his edge a notch. He rose on his own count.",
      houndmaster: "He knelt in the snow and the broken lanterns sent their lights drifting in to him. Cut one on the road and it flies home and lights its lantern again. One that reaches him, a notch. Cut the lot. He got up on his own.",
    } },
    'The Last Vigil': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He knelt once more, and rose to give all three blows in a row. I read each next stance off his blade while the last lines were still landing, and went where it said. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      gloomsworn: "He knelt a last time, as a guard does when the watch is ending, and rose to give every blow he owns, one on the next. His blade named each as the last landed. We read it and moved. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      thresher: "He kneels one more time, dear, and then it's every blow he owns, one after another, the next pose shown just as the last one lands. Read the pose, not the floor, and keep your feet about you. Then a dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      bloodreaver: "He knelt again, and then came every blow he owns, back to back! I watched the blade for the next one while the last still stung, and moved on it. After that, the dance, begun from each of us in turn. A fine finish, my friend.",
      runeguard: "He kneels a last time, and the three blows follow in one breath, each stance shown as the previous one lands. Read the blade, not the floor. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      gorgemaw: "He knelt, and the last course was every dish he has, served one on the next, the next plate shown on his blade as the last was cleared. I read the card and moved. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn. Not a crumb left.",
      oathbroker: "A final vigil is declared, and the three instruments follow in one sitting, each notice entered on his blade as the last takes effect. Act on the notice, not the floor. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      ironpalm: "He knelt one last time, and then spoke three words without a pause. His blade showed each next word as the last was ending. I heard it and moved. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      ranger: "Last kneel. Then all three shots, back to back, next stance shown on the blade as the last lands. Read it, don't wait on the floor. After that, the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      shadowblade: "He knelt a last time, then gave every blow he owns without a pause, each stance shown on his blade as the last one landed. I was gone before the floor lit. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      pyromancer: "He knelt AGAIN and then threw every blow he has, one on top of the next, each pose shown on his blade while the last was still glowing! I moved on the pose. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      thermist: "He kneels, then every blow in a row, each stance shown as the last lands. Move on the blade, not the floor. Then the dance after it, begun from each of us in turn, quick, quick, quicker, no breath between.",
      driftblade: "He knelt once more and then every blow he has came one after another, each stance showing on his blade before the last had finished, so I ran through the end of one and into the start of the next, and then the dance, through the gaps between its lines.",
      marionettist: "The finale: our star kneels, then plays every scene he has without a pause, each pose shown as the last one ends. The company moves on the pose, not the lights. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn. (Encore, denied.)",
      cleric: "He knelt one last time, and then every blow he has came in a row, the next shown on his blade as the last one fell. I called everyone to move on the blade, not the floor, and mended after. Then the dance.",
      grovekeeper: "He knelt, and then every season he has came at once, one right behind the next, each shown on his blade as the last was fading. Read the blade, not the floor, my lot. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      chronomancer: "He kneels, and then all three blows come in a row, the next stance showing as the last lands. We moved on the stance, a beat early. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn. I was ready; I usually am.",
      alembist: "He kneels, then gives every blow in a row. Read the stance on his blade as the last one lands, not the floor. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn. Mend after, never during.",
      threadweaver: "He knelt, and all three blows ran together like one long seam, each stance shown on his blade as the last stitch was pulled. I told my stitches to move on the stance. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      tidecaller: "He knelt, and the water drew back, then every wave he owns came in a row, each shown on his blade as the last one broke. We moved on the swell, not the floor. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      lanternkeeper: "He knelt, and my lanterns flared, and then every blow he owns came one after another, each stance shown on his blade as the last landed. I carried our light by the blade, not the floor. Then the dance.",
      kenseier: "He knelt, and then three strokes without a rest, each next stance shown on his edge as the last lands. A master's finish. Move on the stance, before the floor. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
      houndmaster: "He knelt one more time, then threw every blow he has, back to back, next stance on the blade as the last landed. Moved the lot of us on the stance, dogs too. Then the dance, begun from each of us in turn.",
    } },
    'Last Light of the Pass': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He ran out of patience, and the last light of the pass fell on every one of us. A shield is no use against it. Every blow he landed pushed that hour nearer, and we were not finished in time. A cleaner fight is a shorter fight.",
      gloomsworn: "The last light went out and he was done waiting. Each time we fed his blade, the night ran longer, and there is only so much night. I know when a watch is ending. We ran out of night before we ran out of him.",
      thresher: "At the end he stops waiting, dear, and the light goes out of the pass, and there is no more tidying. Every hit he landed made the fight longer, so be done in time. He keeps his appointment, and we should keep ours.",
      bloodreaver: "The last light went out and he hit every one of us at once, and nobody got up from that one! We had let him have too many notches, and every extra unleashing was more time he had. Be quicker than the notches.",
      runeguard: "The last light of the pass goes out, and the end is the end. Do not feed him, for every extra unleashing pushes the rest of the fight later toward a deadline that does not move.",
      gorgemaw: "The last course came cold to every one of us, and none survived the digestif. Every blow we took meant another course before the dessert, and the kitchen closed. Be finished before the last light.",
      oathbroker: "The term expires: the last light of the pass, fixed in advance. Each extra unleashing we earned pushed our performance later, and the date does not move. We defaulted. Earn fewer of them next time.",
      ironpalm: "He ran out of words, and the last light of the pass went out over all of us. We replied too late. Every blow he landed was another word he got to say. End it before the light goes.",
      ranger: "Last light. Whole pass goes dark and nothing walks out. Every hit that landed fed him, and every unleashing ate the clock. Finish it before then. Nothing left to spare.",
      shadowblade: "The last light went out and the pass filled with dark, and not even I could hide in it. We had fed him, and the fight ran long. What a stickler for the hour. Next time, give him nothing, and be gone before it.",
      pyromancer: "The last light of the pass went OUT, and so did we! Every hit we gave him was another unleashing and another delay, and nothing about it was bright. Be quick, be clean, and be done before it.",
      thermist: "Last light. The whole pass goes cold and dark at once, and that is that. A slow fight, that. Every hit we fed him pushed the end later. Give him nothing, and finish early.",
      driftblade: "The last light went out and the dark came down all at once and there was nowhere to run, and I ran anyway, and it caught me, because we had fed him and the fight had run long, and the dark does not wait.",
      marionettist: "The house lights went out, the final curtain came down on the wrong side, and we were still on stage. We had given him too many encores, and the curtain does not wait. (A bad review.)",
      cleric: "The last light of the pass went out, and not one of us got up. Each mistake had bought him another unleashing, and each unleashing had pushed the end nearer. I should have scolded them harder. Forgive me; I was too gentle.",
      grovekeeper: "The last light of the pass went out like a late frost, and everything died at once. Every mistake let the season run on, and he does not wait. Next time we finish early, and give him nothing to grow on.",
      chronomancer: "The last light goes out at the same hour, however many times we undo it. Every mistake we made pushed the rest of the fight later, toward it. We were too late. Next time, which was last time, we won't be.",
      alembist: "The last light of the pass comes at a fixed hour. Every hit we fed him bought an extra unleashing, which pushed the fight later. Be clean, and be done early; this brew spoils.",
      threadweaver: "The last light went out and every thread was cut at once. We had let too many snags go by, and each one bought him another unleashing and pushed us later. Next time, a tidy seam and an early finish.",
      tidecaller: "The last light went out like the tide going out for good, and everything with it. We had fed him, and the fight ran long, past the turn of the water. A short fight and a clean one, next time.",
      lanternkeeper: "The last light of the pass went out, and he was done waiting. Every mistake we made bought him another unleashing and put the end of our fight later. We ran out of oil. Next time, nothing to feed him, and the lamps still warm.",
      kenseier: "The last light comes without a count, at a fixed hour. Every blow he landed lengthened the fight toward it. We ran out of time before he ran out of strokes. Next time I keep the clock.",
      houndmaster: "Last light of the pass and the whole lot of us went out with it. Fed him too many notches, he unleashed too often, and the fight ran long. Should've been finished first.",
    } },
    'False Stance': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights he shifted his stance halfway through the wind-up. The floor shows only the stance he ends on. I waited for the floor, not the first stance. The line moved once, and moved right.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights the stance changes in the middle of it. A false one first, then the true. I waited for the floor to be painted and trusted nothing else. The dead have learned to distrust a first impression.",
      thresher: "On the hard night he changes his stance in the middle, dear, quite cheekily, so don't trust the first one. The floor is only painted for the stance he ends on, so wait for it.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard nights he lied with his stance! Started one thing and shifted to another halfway, the sneak. The floor only shows the true one. Trust the floor, not the stance. I lost the first bet. A good scar.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights the omen itself deceives: the stance shifts halfway through the wind-up. The floor is painted only for the stance he ends on. The answer is to wait for the floor, and trust nothing before it.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights the menu changes mid-course. He begins one dish, and finishes another. The floor is painted only for the dish he serves. We waited for the plate, not the promise. A rude kitchen.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, the stance is amended mid-instrument, and the floor is painted only for the final text. Rely on the floor and nothing else. We waited for it.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights he began one word and said another. The floor showed only the second. I did not answer the first. I answered the floor.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: he changes stance halfway through the wind-up. Floor only shows the last one. Don't move on the first stance. Wait for the floor.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the mark changes its stance halfway, which is cheating. The floor is painted only for the stance he ends on. I waited for the floor, not for his pose. Cheating deserves patience.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights he changes his stance in the middle, the swindler! The floor glows only for the stance he ends on, so I trust the glow and not the pose. I got it wrong exactly once, in front of everybody.",
      thermist: "Hard nights: the stance shifts halfway. The floor only shows the last one. Don't commit on the first. I did, once, flat out, and ran straight into it. Wait for the floor.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights he changes his stance halfway through, and I ran at the first and was wrong, and so I learned to wait for the floor, which shows only the stance he ends on, and then ran through that.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights our star changes costume in the middle of the scene! The floor is painted only for the final costume. The company learned to wait for the lights, not the pose. (Spoilers, on stage.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights he shifts his stance midway, and the floor is painted only for the last one. I told everyone to wait for the floor, not the pose. It is a cruel trick, and it cost us once.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights the weather turns halfway through the wind-up: he starts one stance and ends in another. Only the floor tells the truth. Wait for it, my lot, and plant after. Don't believe the first sky.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the first stance is not the true one; he shifts halfway through. The floor is painted only for the last. Next time, which was last time, I wait for the floor, and I won't have moved too early.",
      alembist: "On hard nights the stance changes midway, so the first one is a false label. Wait for the floor, which is painted only for the last. Never reach for a flask by its first label.",
      threadweaver: "On hard nights he changes his stance in the middle, and the floor shows only the last. I told my stitches to wait for the floor and not the pose. One sloppy seam cost us, and I mended it after.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights he changes his stance halfway, like a squall turning in the wrong direction. The floor is painted only for the last stance. I held the water back, and so did the others. Patience paid.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights he shifts his stance halfway through. The floor is only painted for the stance he ends on, so I waited for the glow on the ground before I moved a lamp. One lamp early is one lamp wasted.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights the first stance is a feint. The floor is painted only for the stance he ends on, and a blade learns not to answer a feint. We waited for the floor. A good trick; I nodded to him.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, he shifts his stance halfway through. Floor only shows the last one. Held the dogs back till it showed. Sit. Don't trust the first pose.",
    } },
    'Comet Volley': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights, three dashes, not one. Each goes through whoever stands farthest from where he last stopped. The lanterns shelter once each, so I laid out who went where before it began. The line moved on in order.",
      gloomsworn: "Three dashes, on the hard nights, each at whoever stands farthest from where he stopped last. Each lantern shelters once. We watched the lanterns go, one at a time, and nobody took the third.",
      thresher: "On the hard night he dashes three times in a row, dear, each at whoever is farthest from where the last ended. The lanterns shelter once each. Plan the order, and do keep your hat on.",
      bloodreaver: "Three dashes! Each through the one farthest from where he stopped last, and the lanterns shelter once and crack. I wanted to take one on the chin, but the plan said move, and I moved. No scar, which stung in its own way.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights the dash comes three times, each through whoever stands farthest from where the last ended. The lanterns shelter once each. The answer is to settle the order of cover before it begins.",
      gorgemaw: "A tasting menu of three dashes, each through the guest farthest from where the last ended. The lanterns are plates that serve once each. We rationed them and ate nothing. Rich, if rude.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, three dashes are executed in sequence, each against the party farthest from where the last ended. Each lantern's shelter clause applies once only. The order of cover was agreed in advance.",
      ironpalm: "Three dashes, on the hard nights, one after another. Each through the one farthest away. I did not rush. I counted the lanterns, once each, and moved to the next as the last was spent.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: three dashes. Each through whoever's farthest from where the last stopped. Lanterns shelter once each. Plan the cover before it starts. Three runs, three covers.",
      shadowblade: "Three dashes on the hard nights, each through whoever is farthest from where he last stopped. The lanterns shelter once each. I kept near the middle, where I am never the farthest. Three dashes, and not one found me.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights he dashes THREE times, each through whoever is farthest from where he last stopped! The lanterns shelter once each, and I used mine and was absolutely thrilled. Do plan the order first.",
      thermist: "Three dashes, each through the one farthest from where the last ended. Lanterns shelter once each. Plan the order. I ran the last one hot on a lantern's heels, cracking behind me.",
      driftblade: "Three dashes in a row on the hard nights, each through whoever is farthest from where the last ended, and I ran from lantern to lantern through the snow and each sheltered me once and cracked behind me.",
      marionettist: "A triple entrance on the hard nights: three dashes, each through the farthest of the company from the last. The lanterns shelter once each, like props. We blocked the order in advance. (A very short run.)",
      cleric: "Three dashes on the hard nights, each through whoever stood farthest from where he last stopped. The lanterns shelter once each, and I made sure we all knew in what order. Everyone took a turn behind a lantern.",
      grovekeeper: "Three dashes on the hard nights, like three gusts of the same storm, each through the one farthest from the last stop. The lanterns shelter once each. We planned who stood where, and nobody was caught.",
      chronomancer: "Three dashes, a beat apart, each through the one farthest from where the last ended. The lanterns shelter once each. Next time, which was last time, I had the order of cover agreed in advance.",
      alembist: "On hard nights, three dashes in a row, each through the one farthest from the last. Plan the order of lantern cover in advance; each shelters once. Do not run out of lanterns before the third.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights he dashes three times, each through whoever is farthest from where the last ended. The lanterns shelter once each, so I planned the order of my stitches before it began, and not one was caught.",
      tidecaller: "Three swells in a row on the hard nights, each through whoever was farthest from where the last broke. The lanterns shelter once each, and we planned the order.",
      lanternkeeper: "Three dashes on the hard nights, each through whoever stood farthest from the last stop. Each stone lantern sheltered one of us, once, and cracked. Three runs, three lights, and I told each of us where to go.",
      kenseier: "Three dashes in a row on the hard nights, each through the farthest of us from the last ending. The lanterns shelter once each. A courteous trio. We planned the order of cover beforehand.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, three dashes, one after another, each at whoever's farthest from where the last ended. Lanterns shelter once each. Planned who went where beforehand. Rust and Ash stayed off every line.",
    } },
    'Overwhet': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights, a hit taken while he unleashed was kept, and a second edge followed straight after. No ground given in the dance. The line stood clear of everything, start to end.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights, a notch gained mid-unleashing is not lost; it comes back as a second unleashing straight after. I kept the watch clean through the first, and the second never came.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, a hit taken during an unleashing is kept, and another follows straight after the first. So give him nothing all the way through to the end.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard nights he kept every notch earned while he unleashed, and gave us a second right after! I could have asked for nothing better. But the others bleed worse than I do, so we took no hits in the middle of it.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights, notches earned during an unleashing are kept, and one left over fires another straight after the first. Take no hit from the first stance to the last chill.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights a bite taken mid-course is kept, and a second helping follows straight after. We cleaned our plates through the first and the second never came. A small mercy, well earned.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, a notch earned during an unleashing is carried, and a second unleashing is called in straight after the first. No party takes a hit from the first stance to the last chill.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights, a hit taken while he spoke was kept, and he spoke again at once. We took none in the middle of it. He did not speak twice.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: hits taken during an unleashing are kept. Enough of them and he goes again straight after. Take none from first stance to last chill. No second.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights, a mistake made mid-unleashing is kept, and he goes again straight after. So I made none, from the first stance to the last chill. A slip is a gift to him, and I gave none.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights, a hit taken during an unleashing is kept, and he unleashes AGAIN straight after! I gave him nothing from the first stance to the last chill, a feat of discipline I'd like noted.",
      thermist: "Hard nights: hits taken mid-unleashing are kept, and one over the top fires a second. Take none, from first stance to last chill. I came close, and my pulse knew it.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights he kept every hit we took mid-dance and went again straight after, so I ran clean all the way through the first and out the other side, and there was no second, thank the wind.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights a flubbed cue during the finale is kept, and the finale begins again straight after. The company took no hits between the first stance and the last chill. (No encore. Good.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights, a hurt taken while he unleashed was kept against us, and a second unleashing came straight after. I begged everyone to be careful from the first stance to the last chill, and they were.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights, what we let him have mid-unleashing is kept, and a second growth follows straight after. We gave him nothing from the first stance to the last chill. No second season.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights a hit taken mid-unleashing is kept, and he goes again right after the first. We took none from first stance to last chill. I had it all along. There was no second. Nice.",
      alembist: "On hard nights, notches earned during an unleashing are kept, and a leftover one fires a second straight after. Take no hits from first stance to last chill. Never spill mid-brew.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights, a snag taken mid-unleashing is kept, and he goes again straight after the first. I told my stitches to take no hit, from the first stance to the last chill. There was no second blow.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights every blow we let land while he unleashed was kept, and a second swell rolled in behind the first. Nothing landed on us this time, from the first stance until the last chill.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights, a hit taken mid-unleashing is kept, and he goes again straight after. I kept every light where it was needed from the first stance to the last chill, and nobody was caught.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights a notch earned mid-unleashing is kept, and a second edge follows straight after the first. We took none from the first stance to the last chill. Nobody was caught.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, a hit taken while he unleashes is kept, and he goes again straight after. Took none, start to finish, dogs included. No second go.",
    } },
    'Returning Parting': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the hard nights, the dance came back the other way, through wherever we had moved to. Having dodged the first, we dodged the second, on the new ground. Nobody stood still after the first cut.",
      gloomsworn: "On the hard nights, the dance returned, backwards, through wherever we had gone. A man who has stepped out of one grave knows better than to stand in the second. I moved again, off the new lines.",
      thresher: "On the hard night, dear, the dance comes back the other way, through wherever you stepped to. So don't settle in your safe spot. Step off the new lines too, and keep stepping.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard nights he danced it again backwards, through wherever I'd run to! Cheeky. I stepped off the new lines as well, and we left the snow cut up and the party whole.",
      runeguard: "On the harder nights the dance returns, in reverse, through wherever each of us moved to. The safe ground of the first pass is no safe ground at all; read the new lines and step off them as well.",
      gorgemaw: "On the hard nights the dish comes back, reversed, through wherever we sat down after the first. Do not settle. Read the new lines and stand clear of them too. A second helping of the same knife.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms the instrument is executed a second time, in reverse, against the positions we occupy after the first. No position is to be assumed safe. Each party reads the new lines and stands clear.",
      ironpalm: "On hard nights, he spoke it again, backwards, through wherever we had gone. I did not settle. I read the new lines and stepped off them too.",
      ranger: "Hard nights: runs again in reverse, through wherever you moved. Safe spot from the first pass is not safe. Read the new lines. Step off again.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard nights the dance returned, reversed, through wherever we had slipped to, which I thought was rude. I didn't settle. I read the new lines and slipped off them too. Once is craft; twice is routine.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard nights he danced it AGAIN, backwards, through wherever I'd moved to! I had only just stopped being indignant. I read the new lines and moved again. Very put out.",
      thermist: "Hard nights: the dance returns, reversed, through wherever we moved. The first safe spot is not safe. Step off the new lines. I had stopped to catch my breath, and my face went hot at once. Keep moving.",
      driftblade: "On the hard nights the dance came back the other way, through wherever I'd run to, so I never stopped, I read the new lines and cut across them and off the far side, and that is the way to dance.",
      marionettist: "On the hard nights, the reprise: the same dance, reversed, through wherever the company has moved to. Nobody holds a pose. Read the new lines and exit them, stage left. (A very demanding encore.)",
      cleric: "On the harder nights the dance returned, reversed, through wherever we had moved to. I reminded everyone that the safe ground of the first pass is no refuge from the second. Step off the new lines.",
      grovekeeper: "On the hard nights the dance comes back the other way, through wherever we've rooted since. Don't put down roots in the first safe spot, my lot. Read the new lines and step off them too.",
      chronomancer: "On the hard nights the dance comes back, reversed, through wherever we are after the first. Move again off the new lines. I will have known, I did, and I'd already moved.",
      alembist: "On hard nights the dance runs again in reverse, against our new positions. The first safe spot is not safe. Read the new lines and step off them. Nothing should sit still, flask or friend.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard nights the dance comes back reversed, through wherever my stitches have moved to. No settling in the first neat spot. Read the new lines and step off them as well.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights the dance returns on the ebb, backwards, through wherever we have moved. The first safe ground is not safe the second time. Read the new lines and step off them.",
      lanternkeeper: "On the hard nights the dance returned, backwards, through wherever we had moved to. I picked our light up and set it down again, off the new lines, and they followed. The first safe place is not safe twice.",
      kenseier: "On the hard nights the stroke returns, reversed, through wherever we stand after the first. A second count, on new ground. Read the new lines and stand off them. A fine reprise, and honest about it.",
      houndmaster: "Hard nights, the dance ran again, backwards, through wherever we'd moved to. Dogs don't settle in the first dry spot, and neither do I. Read the new lines. Move again.",
    } },
  },
};
